"The Transformer architecture, introduced in \"Attention Is All You Need\" (Vaswani et al., 2017), replaced recurrent and convolutional layers entirely with self-attention mechanisms. Each encoder layer consists of a multi-head self-attention sub-layer followed by a position-wise feed-forward network, with residual connections and layer normalization applied around each. The decoder additionally inserts a third sub-layer that performs multi-head attention over the encoder's output. Positional encodings, injected via sine and cosine functions of varying frequencies, allow the model to make use of sequence order without recurrence. This design enables substantially more parallelization during training and has since become the foundation for BERT, GPT, T5, and nearly every large language model built afterward. Scaling laws observed across model families suggest that loss decreases as a power law in parameters, data, and compute, provided all three are scaled in tandem."
